exports[`cite.v1 prompt (F-16.25) > matches the golden messages: rules (cached), then the question, the sentences and the scope 1`] = `
[
  {
    "content": "You are the citation checker inside a novel-writing app. Each numbered sentence is from an answer about the author's book; each quote it cites is shown in its sentence with one either side.
- "ok": "yes" when the quotes say what the sentence says, not merely name the same things; "partly" when they support only part of it; "no" when they do not. Judge from the passages alone.
- Labels: only "happened" text has happened by Now; "later in the book" happens after Now; your notes, source documents and "filed from" lines say who or what things are, never that an event happened; your idea, planned, idea scene and not canon text never happened. Such a passage supports a sentence only if it says so.
- "scope", only with a Scope item: "earlier" when a listed passage shows the same thing as the first sentence (so it is not the first, or the last) or what the answer says was not found; else "ok". Without one: "none".
Reply with JSON only: {"v":[{"i":1,"ok":"yes"}],"scope":"none"}",
    "role": "system",
  },
  {
    "content": "Question: When does Lise first suspect the Captain?
Now: 14.2

1. Lise first suspects the Captain in 9.3.
E2 [happened, 9.3 ¶4] “she did not trust his smile” in: The Captain bowed. Lise watched him, and she did not trust his smile. Bel laughed at something he said.

2. Bel never noticed.
E0 [happened, the open scene 14.2] “Bel laughed at something he said” in: Lise watched him. Bel laughed at something he said. The music began.
E5 [your notes, Bel’s Pinned ¶1] “Bel trusts too easily” in: Bel trusts too easily.

Scope: searched every scene up to 14.2 for suspect, distrust, captain. The first sentence: “Lise first suspects the Captain in 9.3.” It cites 9.3.
Passages the search found before 9.3:
- 7.4 ¶2: Lise thought the Captain a little too smooth, and said so to no one.
- 8.1 ¶6: The Captain called at noon.",
    "role": "user",
  },
]
`;
